  annotation "note_1771575306641_1", anchor: "a rhyme that invariably reaches eighteen despite mathematical inconsistency, provoking visible concern among adults who will not discuss it", text: "Again. See the Forgotten Shore entry — the most accurate perimeter map of an active corruption zone exists in a children's counting game. See my annotation on Kelm's Dark Verses, where fire-cores appear as domestic commodities an era too early. Tunnel-chicks encode what the Shadow Parliament redacts. Eighteen. I have checked the Parliament's sealed session indices: eighteen is the number of deep-forge access points originally mapped before the Drowned Ledge closure. The adults' concern is not superstition. The children are counting doors.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575306641_2", anchor: "Fire-core braziers still burn through frozen windows; thirty-seven names on the memorial wall", text: "Standard fire-cores radiate warmth for months before exhaustion. The Drowned Ledge was sealed three generations ago. Either someone is resupplying the braziers through the seals — which would require passing the upper locks where someone still leaves offerings — or these are not standard fire-cores. Thirty-seven names. Not round. I trust it.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575306641_3", anchor: "Something non-human returned from those depths communicating in hunt-song frequencies", text: "Buried between a location table and a trade summary. The placement is deliberate. There is no subsection in the apparatus of an encyclopedia entry that can hold this sentence at its actual weight.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575306641_4", anchor: "After the dissolution, the Claws' territorial control fragmented; during the Faction Wars", text: "The dissolution is recent. The Faction Wars are not. This paragraph moves backward in time without signaling the shift. The ☽'ofund' Guild interdiction belongs to the Faction Wars era, when the Claws were tunnel gangs, not the post-dissolution period the preceding sentence describes. My error in sequencing, not the ice's — I was pulling from shadow-ledger sources that organize by territorial claim rather than chronology, and the seam shows.", type: :temporal, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575306641_5", anchor: "marked with symbols in ash or blood by hands no living species claims", text: "At depth-mark 7 in the Foundation archive — below the administrative ice, below the Clever Ice Age strata, below everything I have been funded to catalogue — there are impressions that are not penguin and are not orca. They are shaped by anatomy I cannot reconstruct from the marks alone. The symbols described here are consistent with what I have recorded at that depth: structured, deliberate, and made by something that understood what it was writing. I have included this observation in no prior report because no prior report asked the right question. This entry, which I wrote, does not ask it either. I have placed the evidence in the main text and left the question in the margin, which is perhaps the most honest thing I have done in this edition.", type: :tangent, display: :full
